# ⛔⛔ THE WEB SERVICE'S IMAGE — and the ONLY image in this repo that carries OCR.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Four services — web, worker, flow-worker and bars-api —
# are built from the SAME repository root and differ only at start time, by
# environment variable, inside one shared `railway.json`. Wave P needs Tesseract
# on web and nowhere else: worker, flow-worker and bars-api cannot perform OCR,
# do not reach the Notebook database, and must never install a package they will
# never invoke.
#
# ⚰️ THE MECHANISM THIS REPLACES. The first attempt kept web on nixpacks and
# pointed it at a second config file with a `NIXPACKS_CONFIG_FILE` service
# variable. The variable REACHED THE CONTAINER and did not select the build:
# web's build config path is a first-class field of the Railway service manifest
# (`nixpacksConfigPath`, measured as `/nixpacks.toml`), not something a service
# env var overrides. The build succeeded, and Tesseract was simply absent.
#   ⛔ A RUNTIME VARIABLE BEING PRESENT DOES NOT PROVE IT SELECTED THE BUILD
#   GRAPH. Runtime service scoping ≠ build-config selection.
#
# ⭐ THE PRECEDENT. `services/chart_renderer/` already proves this project
# supports an independently built service whose system dependencies differ —
# it is `builder: DOCKERFILE` while the rest are nixpacks (and bars-api is
# railpack: three builders already coexist here). This is the same move for web,
# with one difference that matters: chart-renderer is a tiny standalone app
# uploaded from its own directory, while web is built from the repository root
# and must keep EVERY dependency it has today.
#
# ⛔ HOW WEB SELECTS THIS FILE: `railway.web.json` (repo-owned) declares
# `builder: DOCKERFILE` + `dockerfilePath: Dockerfile.web`, and web's service
# setting names that file as its config-as-code path. The other three keep
# reading `railway.json`, which still says NIXPACKS. THE FILE IS NAMED
# `Dockerfile.web`, NOT `Dockerfile`, on purpose — a root `Dockerfile` is
# auto-detected, and would be a landmine for any service that ever loses its
# explicit builder setting.
#
# ⛔ PARITY IS THE RISK, NOT SIZE. A build boundary that quietly drops a
# dependency is worse than no boundary at all, and the drop would not show up
# for weeks: `node` is invoked at RUNTIME (COT narrative prewarm, Friday 17:05
# ET) and `ffmpeg` at RUNTIME (Desk background-audio extraction). Measured
# inventory of the live nixpacks image, 2026-09-08:
#     python 3.12.7 (venv at /opt/venv, first on PATH)   node v20.18.0
#     ffmpeg 7.1     git      curl      tar/xz/gzip      tesseract ABSENT
# Everything above is reproduced below. `tests/test_ocr_build_isolation.py`
# fails if any of it disappears from this file.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STAGE 1 — the frontend, built the way nixpacks builds it today.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ THE NODE VERSION IS PINNED TO WHAT PRODUCTION RUNS TODAY (v20.18.0), so
# this slice changes packaging and nothing else. Raising it is a deliberate,
# separate decision — worth making, since this tag is from 2024-11 and carries
# no later security patches — but not inside an OCR packaging change.
FROM node:20.18.0-bookworm-slim AS frontend
WORKDIR /src

# ⛔⛔ EVERY `VITE_*` THE FRONTEND READS MUST BE DECLARED HERE OR IT BUILDS
# UNDEFINED. This is the one thing the nixpacks build did for free and a Docker
# build does not: nixpacks ran `npm run build` with the SERVICE's environment, so
# Railway's `VITE_*` variables were present in `process.env` and Vite inlined
# them. A Docker stage inherits nothing — Railway offers each service variable
# as a BUILD ARG, and an undeclared build arg is silently dropped.
#
# ⚰️ WHAT THAT COST, measured on the deployed bundle 2026-09-12. Nine `VITE_*`
# variables were set on `web`, eight of them to the literal `1`, and ALL NINE were
# undefined in the shipped JS from `af80e0b91` (2026-09-08 21:53 ET) onward:
#
#   VITE_COMING_SOON            the COMING SOON holding page never rendered —
#                               `/` served the real Landing page and the whole
#                               marketing funnel was public, while the backend's
#                               COMING_SOON_MODE still refused every signup
#   VITE_REALTIME_BARS          the Massive bars push feed never subscribed; every
#                               chart silently fell back to the Finnhub poll
#   VITE_FLOW_PARTS             Options Flow fetched whole-D + the full tape
#                               instead of `part=bootstrap` + `part=TOP_PICKS`
#   VITE_FLOW_DEFER_TAPE        the tape was never deferred
#   VITE_FLOW_SERVER_SEARCH     search ran client-side over the whole tape
#   VITE_FLOW_SERVER_TOPPICKS   top picks were recomputed in the browser
#   VITE_MASSIVE_STREAM         the Live Flow stream rollout never engaged
#   VITE_DESK_BG_AUDIO_ENABLED  desk background audio stayed off
#   VITE_CHART_RENDER_TOKEN     folded to `''`, so the `/r/*` renderers' CLIENT
#                               token check (`if (TOKEN && …)`) never ran
#
# ⭐ NOTHING FAILED. A flag read as `=== '1'` is false when undefined, so every
# one of these is indistinguishable from "deliberately off" at every layer a test
# or a health check can see — the suite stayed green and `/api/health` stayed 200.
# The only witness is the built artifact. That is why the rail
# (`tests/test_dockerfile_vite_build_args.py`) DERIVES this list from `app/src/**`
# rather than trusting anyone to keep a hand-typed copy current.
#
# ⚠️ `ENV` as well as `ARG` on purpose. Docker does expose build args to `RUN`,
# but that is one sentence of documentation standing between a member and a
# silently disabled product; the `ENV` makes it true under either reading. This
# stage is discarded by the multi-stage build, so nothing lands in the runtime
# image. An unpassed one becomes `""`, which changes nothing: `'' === '1'` is
# false exactly as `undefined === '1'` is.
ARG VITE_CATALYST_UI_ENABLED
ARG VITE_CHART_RENDER_TOKEN
ARG VITE_COMING_SOON
ARG VITE_DESK_BG_AUDIO_ENABLED
ARG VITE_DISCORD_CHART_APP_ID
ARG VITE_FLOW_DEFER_TAPE
ARG VITE_FLOW_PARTS
ARG VITE_FLOW_SERVER_SEARCH
ARG VITE_FLOW_SERVER_TOPPICKS
ARG VITE_GRID_WARM_ENABLED
ARG VITE_LAUNCH_DATE
ARG VITE_MASSIVE_CURATED_STREAM
ARG VITE_MASSIVE_STREAM
ARG VITE_PICOVOICE_ACCESS_KEY
ARG VITE_REALTIME_BARS
ARG VITE_TWITTER_UI_ENABLED
ARG VITE_WS_HOST
ENV VITE_CATALYST_UI_ENABLED=$VITE_CATALYST_UI_ENABLED \n    VITE_CHART_RENDER_TOKEN=$VITE_CHART_RENDER_TOKEN \n    VITE_COMING_SOON=$VITE_COMING_SOON \n    VITE_DESK_BG_AUDIO_ENABLED=$VITE_DESK_BG_AUDIO_ENABLED \n    VITE_DISCORD_CHART_APP_ID=$VITE_DISCORD_CHART_APP_ID \n    VITE_FLOW_DEFER_TAPE=$VITE_FLOW_DEFER_TAPE \n    VITE_FLOW_PARTS=$VITE_FLOW_PARTS \n    VITE_FLOW_SERVER_SEARCH=$VITE_FLOW_SERVER_SEARCH \n    VITE_FLOW_SERVER_TOPPICKS=$VITE_FLOW_SERVER_TOPPICKS \n    VITE_GRID_WARM_ENABLED=$VITE_GRID_WARM_ENABLED \n    VITE_LAUNCH_DATE=$VITE_LAUNCH_DATE \n    VITE_MASSIVE_CURATED_STREAM=$VITE_MASSIVE_CURATED_STREAM \n    VITE_MASSIVE_STREAM=$VITE_MASSIVE_STREAM \n    VITE_PICOVOICE_ACCESS_KEY=$VITE_PICOVOICE_ACCESS_KEY \n    VITE_REALTIME_BARS=$VITE_REALTIME_BARS \n    VITE_TWITTER_UI_ENABLED=$VITE_TWITTER_UI_ENABLED \n    VITE_WS_HOST=$VITE_WS_HOST
# The whole tree, because the build reaches outside `app/src`: `npm run build`
# is `vite build` PLUS two esbuild bundles (`scripts/build-cot-facts.mjs`,
# `scripts/build-flow-facts.mjs`) whose outputs the BACKEND shells out to.
COPY . .
RUN cd app && npm install && NODE_OPTIONS='--max-old-space-size=4096' npm run build

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STAGE 2 — python dependencies, compiled where a compiler is allowed to exist.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything in requirements.txt ships manylinux wheels for cp312 today, so this
# stage normally compiles nothing. It exists so that the FIRST package that
# stops shipping a wheel fails a rebuild instead of failing at 3am — and so the
# toolchain never reaches the runtime image.
FROM python:3.12-slim-bookworm AS pydeps
ENV PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 \
    PIP_DISABLE_PIP_VERSION_CHECK=1
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        build-essential \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
# `--copies`, and at /opt/venv, exactly as the nixpacks install phase does it:
# `railway.json`'s start command runs a BARE `uvicorn` / `python -m api.…`, so
# the venv must be on PATH under that path or nothing starts.
RUN python -m venv --copies /opt/venv
ENV PATH="/opt/venv/bin:$PATH"
COPY requirements.txt ./
RUN pip install --upgrade pip && pip install -r requirements.txt

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STAGE 3 — the runtime image.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FROM python:3.12-slim-bookworm AS runtime
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 \
    LANG=C.UTF-8 \
    LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 \
    PATH="/opt/venv/bin:$PATH"

# ⛔ APT, NOT A DOWNLOAD (§9). The language data is a package, so nothing about
# a boot depends on a third-party fetch succeeding.
#   tesseract-ocr      the engine + leptonica
#   tesseract-ocr-eng  English traineddata ONLY — no other language, no training
#                      toolchain, no -dev headers
# The four above it are PARITY with the image this replaces, not new appetite.
RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
        ca-certificates \
        curl \
        ffmpeg \
        git \
        libstdc++6 \
        tesseract-ocr \
        tesseract-ocr-eng \
    && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

# ⭐ THE RUNTIME NODE IS THE SAME BINARY THAT BUILT THE FRONTEND — one version,
# one authority, no second tag to drift. `libstdc++6` above is what it links
# against; npm is deliberately NOT carried over, because nothing at runtime
# invokes it (`cot_prewarm` and `flow_aggregate` both exec `node` directly).
COPY --from=frontend /usr/local/bin/node /usr/local/bin/node
COPY --from=pydeps /opt/venv /opt/venv

WORKDIR /app
# The build context is the git tree — `node_modules/`, `.venv/` and `app/dist/`
# are gitignored and never reach it — so this copies what Railway ships today
# and cannot silently omit an API module, a migration, a script or an asset.
COPY . /app
COPY --from=frontend /src/app/dist /app/app/dist

# ⛔ OCR IS DARK. The engine is INSTALLED, not ENABLED: nothing here sets
# `J2_OCR_ENABLED`, so `document_ocr_tesseract.install_if_enabled()` wires no
# adapter and `document_ocr.ocr_available()` stays false. Presence of the binary
# and activation of the feature are two different facts, and only one of them
# is in this file.
#
# `railway.web.json`'s start command is what actually runs — this CMD is the
# same web branch of it, so the image is still correct if run without Railway.
CMD ["sh", "-c", "exec uvicorn api.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port ${PORT:-8080} --proxy-headers --forwarded-allow-ips='*' --timeout-graceful-shutdown 5"]
